useState is how React remembers information inside a component — like a variable that, when it changes, automatically refreshes what's shown on screen.In the AI Kiosk I built, we had a patient's name displayed on screen. When the voice assistant captured the name, I'd do:
const [patientName, setPatientName] = useState('')
Calling setPatientName('Dhanil') would instantly update what's shown.useEffect is for side effects — things that need to happen after the component renders, like fetching data or setting up a WebSocket connection.In the kiosk, I used useEffect to start the WebSocket connection when the component mounted, and clean it up when it was removed — so we didn't have memory leaks.
useEffect(() => {
  const ws = new WebSocket('wss://voice-api/realtime');
  ws.onmessage = (e) => setTranscript(e.data);
  return () => ws.close(); // cleanup on unmount
}, []); // [] means run once on mount

